In article <35e833be.0@news.wizvax.net>, John Wilson <wilson@dbit.com> wrote:
>In article <6s910k$2bo$1@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM>,
>Jordan Henderson <jordan@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM> wrote:
>>I remembered that after posting that the compiler does in fact have
>>support for a 'nul-terminated string' in it's string constants.  There's 
>>another place as well, command line arguments are supported as nul-terminated
>>strings.
>
>Nitpick:  the argc/argv command line arguments *are* set up by the C
>runtime library as part of the init code, so they're not part of the
>core language itself.  But since the C runtime init is normally linked
>in without the user's permission or knowledge it *seems* like it's part
>of the language.
>

I have to admit, I don't have the C standard at hand.  However, I would
be surprised if the standard >required< that command line arguments be setup
by something "linked in".  I believe that a conforming implementation 
could have the compiler writing all the code into the user's program 
necessary to perform this command line argument setup.

I believe that a conforming implementation can write the code in-line
for any Standard Library call (and optimize based on this in-lining).  
This seems to me to make the entire Standard Library part of the 
"core language".

I think that, conceptually, the handling of command line arguments is
part of the core language.  What >is< part of the core language and what is 
not?  And what is your justification for the separation?

