I use C. I like C. I program almost exclusively in C. However:

On 29 Aug 1998 19:49:37 GMT, Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
>Next issue: for the last and final time, neither the standard library,
>nor the run-time init/exit routines, nor command arguments, nor strings,
>are part of C. They have been bundled with it (implemented for one's
>respective operating envionrment) in _most_ instances of a C compiler,
>for a long time now, and as a matter of convention and according to ANSI
>are part of a C development environment. however they are NOT part of the
>actual language! how hard is it for people to understand this? 

A difference that makes no difference is no difference.

Very few programmers use just a C compiler. (Folks writing embedded systems,
and that's about it.) To the rest of the programming world, the language
includes the runtime startup, and the standard library, and string
constants, and everything else. It really doesn't matter where a particular
feature is implemented; it only matters that it is.

Here's an exercise for you, Gregg: Implement a C environment that uses
Pascal-style counted strings instead of null-terminated ones. Try to get it
ANSI-certified. Try to sell it as C. If you are caught, the Secretary will
disavow any knowledge of your actions. Good luck, Mr. Economou.

Now, can we PLEASE quit picking this particular nit?



