Lawrence Kirby wrote:
> The names of standard library functions are reserved as identifiers with
> external linkage. That means that it isn't valid for a user program to
> provide definitions for them. Therefore a compiler is well within its rights
> to inline calls to functions with external linkage that have the name of
> a standard library function.

That is not true. 

1- The list of reserved words does not include function calls.  The compiler
has no idea
   what "printf" is supposed to do. It lets the linker figure out what to do
with that 
   reference. And the parameters given to the linker define where the linker
will look for
   these references.

2- Some compilers are used to generate code for a different target platform.
   For instance, the DOS "TopSpeed" C compiler is used to generate code for the
	PSION handheld computers. Instead of linking against the DOS libraries, it
	links against the PSION supplied libraries, do the reference to "printf" results
	in a call to a different routine which is compatible with the PSION operating system
	(which is not DOS). Both platforms are 80x86 based but run different
operating systems.
	There is no way that the actual compiler on DOS would know how to translate
what a "printf"
	might result in.

3- There are some "includes" which do define macros with the same name as a
standard function
   call. But that is not part of the compiler itself, it is part of the
"package" which includes
   the "includes" and the library calls that are designed to integrate with
the target operating
   system.



