JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
>Lawrence Kirby wrote:
>> The names of standard library functions are reserved as identifiers with
>> external linkage. That means that it isn't valid for a user program to
>> provide definitions for them. Therefore a compiler is well within its rights
>> to inline calls to functions with external linkage that have the name of
>> a standard library function.
>
>That is not true. 

Is to.

>1- The list of reserved words does not include function calls.  

Yes it does.

>The compiler has no idea what "printf" is supposed to do. 

A compiler is allowed to know exactly how printf is supposed to
function.

>It lets the linker figure out what to do with that reference. And the
>parameters given to the linker define where the linker will look for
>these references.

One reason standard library function names are reserved with
external linkage is if you define your own function with a
reserved name, the linker is not going to know whether to link
with your user-defined function or the standard library function.
For example, having two printfs to deal with invokes undefined-
behavoir. 

>2- Some compilers are used to generate code for a different target 
>platform.

[...]

>There is no way that the actual compiler on DOS would know how to
>translate what a "printf" might result in.

A compiler would probably be more limited on what it could do,
but that example doesn't prove what a compiler can or can't
do overall. It is still allowed to inline code and even call
specially optimized versions of printf based on how it's being
used.

>3- There are some "includes" which do define macros with the same
>name as a standard function call. But that is not part of the
>compiler itself, it is part of the "package" which includes the
>"includes" and the library calls that are designed to integrate 
>with the target operating system.

I think it's generally best to think of the standard library
as part of the C language itself. The fact that it may be
implementated seperately really doesn't matter; whether the
preprocessor is part of the compiler or a stand alone utility
really doesn't matter as long as the pakage works correctly.

Also, a macro may get exapanded or a function called based on
how it's being used. That is part of the language.

-- 
