In article <6scnqc$ilb@elf.bsdi.com> I noted that GCC inlines various
"library" calls -- that is, things that look like ordinary function
calls, in C code, turn out not to be function calls at all.

In article <6sei0r$745$1@usenet01.srv.cis.pitt.edu> Gregg E Economou
<geest3+@pitt.edu> writes:
>That's an optimization feature of your compiler,
>not a feature of the underlying language. 

The key question is:  Is this optimization feature allowed by the
language definition (ANSI X3.159 / ISO 9899)?

If you say "no":  Why not?

If you say "yes":  What does that tell us about users redefining the
standard library functions themselves?


For those who do not have the time to look at the standard, and
are willing to believe me, the answer to the "key question" above
is "yes, this optimization feature is in fact allowed by the language
definition".

As an implementor, I must of course supply all those functions that
the compiler does not open-code in this manner.  (I even have to
supply the open-coded memcpy, because someone might call memcpy
through a function pointer.  This function need not be named
"memcpy", though -- since the compiler does in fact recognize
"calls" to memcpy, I might have to supply instead a function named
something like "_Cruntime__stdstringf__copy_nonoverlap", or perhaps
I might have to supply two routines __libc_noerrchk_memcpy and
__libc_errchk_memcpy, for two different compilation environments,
one of which performs runtime error-checking.)

As a user of the C language, however, I should not attempt to
subvert any of the implementation's functions.  If I do, I may
find I have tied myself to a particular set of implementations,
or I might discover that I cannot use the optimization features
of my compiler.

None of this means you *cannot* subvert your compiler.  It just
means that if and when you do, you cannot generalize the result to
"all C compilers".  If I were to claim that supplying your own
memcpy(), that consisted of a call to exit(), never made memcpy
exit, you would probably wonder where I got that idea.  But I can
prove that at least one C program that appears to call this "exiting
memcpy" does in fact continue after the call, on at least one
system, in at least one mode.  "It works that way on my compiler"
does *not* mean "it must work that way everywhere".
