String handling in a language that doesn't support dynamic memory management
(including garbage collection!) is almost impossible to do "right". The best
you can do is use a library. NONE of the libraries that were in use when the
null termination convention was first developed are still in use (the closest,
the Version 6 libc, was obsolete by 1980). If you don't like the way your
library works, change it!

Hell, I've worked on boxes where null terminated and counted strings were
in concurrent use. It's not a big deal.

