>It's not a "design".  It's a common convention among C programmers.
>
>If you want to use Pascal-format strings instead, you're perfectly free
>to -- without any of the intereference that a language that *didn't*
>understand that everything was, after all, just memory accessed
>with a different granularity, would give you.

How do you propose I do this?  Say I write a program:

main() { char *hello = "Hello"; pascal_format_puts(hello); }

How do I get the compiler to generate the hello string as a pascal
string?  Do compilers typically have a command line option to specify
what kind of strings you can use?  In particular, does gcc?

It seems that the only semi-portable way to do this would be to compile
the program with strings constants as writable, and convert each string
a run-time.  This is a serious, serious kludge, and I'd hardly say that
this is a "perfectly free" task (especially since it is likely that
many compilers do not even support writable string constants, so it's
non-portable, which defeats the main purpose of C).

Regards,

Terry Murphy



