On 28 Aug 1998 15:23:13 GMT, tsmurphy@cs.uiuc.edu (TERENCE MURPHY)
wrote in article <6s6i11$jla$1@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>:

>How do you propose I do this?  Say I write a program:
>
>main() { char *hello = "Hello"; pascal_format_puts(hello); }
>
>How do I get the compiler to generate the hello string as a pascal
>string? 

Think of OpenVMS descriptors, and have a pascal_strings.h which
contains ...

 typedef struct
 {
  unsigned int length;
  void *string_ptr; 
 } pascal_string_t;

 #define pascal_string( variable, value )\
  pascal_string_t variable = { strlen( value ), (void *)value };

 void pascal_format_puts( pascal_string_t string );

Then you can have ...

 #include "pascal_string.h"

 main() { pascal_string( hello, "Hello" ); pascal_format_puts( hello ); }

Greg
