> this is a "perfectly free" task (especially since it is likely that
> many compilers do not even support writable string constants, so it's
> non-portable, which defeats the main purpose of C).

As has been mentioned, the definition of a descriptor structure could
be encapsulated in a macro and would result in painless generation of
suitable string constants; and with an optimizing compiler the strlen()
might actually be optimized away so that the run-time cost would be
minimal.

The effort involved in generating alternative string constants, even
without this, turns out to be minor compared to writing and maintaining
replacements to all the run-time routines that currently work with
null-terminated strings only.

May I suggest C++, or, better yet, Java, as the only reasonable way
to write "C" code with safe strings?

