In article <35fbd212.2154018254@news.axion.bt.co.uk>, thomasgd@omc.bt.co.uk (Greg Thomas) writes:
>
>Think of OpenVMS descriptors, and have a pascal_strings.h which
>contains ...
>
> typedef struct
> {
>  unsigned int length;
>  void *string_ptr; 
> } pascal_string_t;

That stores the length, but does not store the maximum length, so 
you're still into buffer overrun land for safe_strcpy.  Better would be:

typedef struct
{
  unsigned int storage_length;
  unsigned int length;
  void *string_ptr; 
} pascal_string_t;

Where storage_length is either set at malloc() time, or if strings are 
packed into one big memory space, shortly thereafter.  So it wastes a bit 
of space in the data, but might end up being more efficient in other ways.

For instance

  status=safe_strcat(safe_string1,safe_string2);

can check the sizes *before* it does anything, and then use
a nice efficient loop to transfer one onto the end of the other.  You
call it, you know you're not going to get a buffer overrun.

Unfortunately, null terminated strings permeate the libraries, so that you
would probably have to resort to making all such strings one character
longer than they would overwise be (storage_length - 1) so that you can pop
a '\0' onto the end before calling printf (or whatever).  That's still 
nasty though - what if somebody passes you a read only safe_string?
To save the extra byte your safe_string.c module would have to implement
dozens, and dozens of functions.

That said, it would not be at all a bad idea to do so, since null
terminated strings seem to be at the root of at least half the problems in
C and C based operating systems. 

