hold on a sec! 
When the compiler sees this, heres what it does. 

In article <6s6i11$jla$1@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
>How do you propose I do this?  Say I write a program:
>
>main() { char *hello = "Hello"; pascal_format_puts(hello); }
>


first, it sees the char declaration and generates a macro for allocating
space at the stack. (or it generates the code itself) and investigates
the ="hello" part to count how much space. On word-aligned systems
or other such thing it figures out how much space to use. Then it ultimately
generates some code which has the effect of pushing future stack references
further by however much space was needed, and has the image (since this is
after all a compiled-in literal) contain the 'hello' in the right place. 
|There is _nothing_ in the C compiler that understands that you wanted a
string. This is because there is no such thing as a 'string' in the C
compiler. When you use a library function like printf to display that
'hello' text, (in this case i am talking about how printf works but
they all do somethign like it) , the function printf is called after putting
the address of that bit of memory where the 'hello' started in a location
that printf will search for its arguments. Usually printf will have its
format string pointed to by a register and then either arguments pointed to
by registers or else the next register will point to an array of pointers
to arguments. that part is irrelevant. Printf, when told to print a string,
will loop through the memory pointed to by its argument, where you told it
there wa sa string, and printf will copy those bytes to the right place and
have some other system calls or i/o calls get them printed out, and it will
cease doing whatevver it takes to print them out when it sees one of them
is zero (null). this is the closest you get to the undertsanding of a 'string'
in this scenario. Your 'dependence' on a null-terminated 'string'  "in C"
is really the fact that printf is implemented to look for things like
that when you tell it to print a string. It also reads its format string
in that fashion. 
So really your dependence on 'strings' being null terminated is because
the function (in this case printf) that you are choosing to use expects
that when you are talking about 'strings' you mean 'a bunch of bytes 
consecutively found in memory the last of which is a null, beginning at
some address'. Those people who things char*blorf makes blorf a string
are misled- char*blorf really means that blorf is a pointer (the compiler
will decide how much space is needed to store an address of the proper
form for whatever system it is making code for) and that the amount of
data that is nominally pointed to is one character, which C specifies to be
no less tha eight bits in length. Since it is an address and no more,
you could of course do somethin glike

int* blarf;
char*blorf=blarf;

and be legal- but the char* declaration hints that wherever you actually
refrence blorf in the source code, corresponding assembly or machine code
will be generated which operates on the refrenced contents as a character,
i.e. on some systems selecting instructons with byte operations as opposed
to word or other such operations, on other systems invoking a mask and shift
to operate on tthe value solely of this byte, and so on and so forth- it
is a directive to hint at what you are referencing with this address
and more than anything else is used to know how far down the stack one
must read to retreive it. it means nothing about a string. In C there is
no such thing as a string. There is memory. thats about all there is in C.
integers, floats, and so on are all just directives to amounts of memory
with a predetermined size. you could just as easily (though many compilers'
preprocessors will complain or forbid this) use any other kind of pointer
and treat it as a 'string' if you wished. However this would have some
ramifications at compile time when things of different sizes were allocated 
and so on. 
oi! sorry to everyone for having to read this hideous ridiculosity, but
for those who didnt know this stuff, now you do and now you know why
a: there is no dependenceon any kind of data tye in C beyond the
specifications that char be >7 bits long, int be >15 bits long, and long
be >31 bits long.
b: the concept of a 'string' in C is a convention which has persisted 
for many reasons but are all human factors in choosing to determine the
end of some text in this manner. 

happy hacking,
gregg


>How do I get the compiler to generate the hello string as a pascal
>string?  Do compilers typically have a command line option to specify
