what do you mean by this? 
if youd liek to make a pascal string of this, then you have to create
that structure somewhere. Your first step will probably be to count the
size of the stuff that you would call a string that starts at the address
contained in the location refrenced by hello, and then copy the data 
which begins at that address and continues to the length you detrermined
into some other place in memory. Then you might continue by creating any
further aspects of the structure you need, and then of course youd need
an implementation of that print-a-pasca-string fucntion you wanted to
call. If its in a library, call it and make sure it
was declared or else extern'ed and either way linked in later. 
Then youd call it, using whatever means it needs to find the pascal
string contruct in memory and oprate on it. 

>
>It seems that the only semi-portable way to do this would be to compile
>the program with strings constants as writable, and convert each string
>a run-time.  This is a serious, serious kludge, and I'd hardly say that
>this is a "perfectly free" task (especially since it is likely that
>many compilers do not even support writable string constants, so it's
>non-portable, which defeats the main purpose of C).

um, no, you are missing the entire point of C and its portability. 
C does NOT purport to provide some hideous monstrosity of massive strucutred
langauges with heavy type enforcement. C is portable because NONE of
that stuff is forced on the programmer. In C, the most important
thing (and the only true data type) is a byte of memory. everythign else
is some quantity of bytes of memory, and the other important thing of
memory is the pointer. You have to know where in memory your stuff is. 
The second important thing in C aside form memory is logic and arithmetic. 
You do all that nifty stuff, including control flow and so on, and all
the time you are performing your operations to process the contents of some
locations of memory, ultimately storing your results somewhere (be it a
register, or using some instruction to send data to the bus, or back to
memory, is beyond the scope of this) and thus effecting some work done,
which is the goal of any useful nontrivial program. the third thing you
must consider with C is the possibility that you dont have all the memory
at your disposal- things like other programs, and perhaps your own stack
and so on, are places you should tread lightly, but are in no way bound
to- you have the right and the means to destroy even yourself, or 
modify even yourself. 
now, many comilers will do all the fun of managing your stack, i mean 
after all thats part of its job, and many compilers have some macros
built in for many things. There is part of C a macro facility, as well,
which is of course the preprocessor defines and such. the preprocessor is
part of a compilation facility but doesnt compile anything per se- it
just processes the source code before compilation. Many people dont see
the distinction well since other 'compilers' have incorporated this
concept much more into the compilation stage and in many cases cannot
be separated from it. Anyway, all the stuff like i/o and system calls
and stuff, none of that is part of C itself. (regardless of whether ANSI
calls the standard library a part of the language, it isnt. it is something
which one can expect any ANSI C compiler to be able to support and operates
as specified by the standard, but it is not part of the language but
rather a set of routines written _in_ C which ANSI decided everyone should
have at their disposal because of their widespready handiness and utility. )
At this point C looks like a nice way to mask the tedium of assembly
rogramming wihtout giving up much of the direct control and without losing
performance. All the higher-level stuff is implemented _in_ C, but is not
_part_ of the language. Only the extreme simplicity of C itself the 
nlanguge, allos such higher level contructs to exist and work. 

I highly suggest the K&R book at this point before i have to start
talking about structs and stuff... :)



happy hacking,
gregg



