why must people consistently fault the tool for what really ought to be
blamed (if they think it is so terrible) on those _using_ the tool. 
First of all, you might see less of these poor unrobust programs from
other languages not because it is more difficult to write bad programs
in other languages, but perhaps because other languages arent useful enough
to do what they needed to do. perhaps? maybe? think about it. 
No i do not want to start a language war. 
Next issue: for the last and final time, neither the standard library,
nor the run-time init/exit routines, nor command arguments, nor strings,
are part of C. They have been bundled with it (implemented for one's
respective operating envionrment) in _most_ instances of a C compiler,
for a long time now, and as a matter of convention and according to ANSI
are part of a C development environment. however they are NOT part of the
actual language! how hard is it for people to understand this? 
Because of the existing convention that the commonly encountered need for
some text to be handled primarily for purposes of i/o, that the location
and size of this text be identified as beginning at some known location
and ending at the next instance of a null, current and future support
for this method of handling a 'string' will exist. Humans are creatures
of habit. But there is no such thing as a string in C. There is no such
thing as a command line argument in C, or a command line. All those
things are conventions dictated by the design of various operating
systems which poeple commonly deal with, and with what facilities the
designers of various hardware environments chose to provide the user with,
and the authors of C comilers subsequently  designed their product around. 
Libraries, including the 'standard' library, are merely archives of 
precompiled object code. Their headers are merely declarations so that
the linker can handle them and that the compiler can allocate necessary
accomodations for them. The librariesin C are written in C, they are not 
part of C. end of story! i cant believe i am still explaining this to 
people!
Other languages which do enforce strict type checking, bounds checking,
control of this and that and the other thing, and have all kinds of anal
hierarchies, have their advantages of 'safety' and 'integrity' but like
all such 'safetey' nets, they do so by restricting, hindering, or outright 
prohibiting the user from certain sets of operations which on the underlying
machine are perfectly legal. This in my humble opinion constitutes a
reduction in the utility of the system by reducing the size of the set
of things i have at my disposal as services of the hardware to perform
the work i want done, and forces me to use a smaller set of rigidly defined
services that someone else thought were a nice thing to have. My 
requirements and goals are not one and the same wiht whoever else came along,
and consequently i will be forced, if i wish to use a rigid and strongly
typed language, to beat my problem into the right shape for the mold
instead of beat the machine into the shape for my problem. That in a 
nutshell is why C has its advantages. Certainly for people who are 
afraid of making a mistake they can go use some other language and
i hope they enjoy it- but dont start accusing C of things that have little
or nothign to do with C!!

