Petri Backstrom <petrib@microsoft.com> writes:
> Both Digital (for Alpha) and HP (for HP-PA) worked with Novell on 
> so called native NetWare server ports a few years back (the code 
> was for most parts up and running and shown at various events such
> as Comdex, etc.).

  Right. As I recall, that was the "portable Netware" project. However,
"portable" meant "written in C" while the PC-based Netware of the time
was mostly in assembler. Or perhaps I'm thinking of one of the earlier
port attempts?

  And of course, file serving in those days (before client/server, etc.)
wasn't very CPU demanding - a lot of sites only upgraded their 286 boxes
because they couldn't get parts any more. I know of lots of sites still
running Netware 3.2 on 486/66 class hardware w/ 16MB.

  Since then, Netware has sort of fizzled - they positioned 4.0 as the
place to be, but even clubbing their users over the head with free money
and other incentives didn't get customers to migrate - even when Novell
charged *less* for Netware 4.x than for 3.x, 3.x still outsold 4.x for
quite some time.

  Not having a single, consistent reference platform made the other plat-
forms less attractive, and many of the people who wanted some degree of
interoperability went with Interconnections, TGV's Multiware, etc. - and
the demise of those products seems to indicate a lack of interest on the
part of many users. I'd explain this by asking: "Given that Netware ser-
vers are a commodity item, why would anyone pay more for an orphan-step-
child version on a DEC or HP CPU?".

  And since then, Netware's market seems to be doing a good vanishing
act - everywhere I turn, I keep hearing "NT". I expect that most of the
legacy sites running Netware 2.x and 3.x w/ old applications will have
to convert/upgrade for Y2K, and my impression is that many won't stay
w/ Netware.

  Of course, Samba does a very good job of providing services to PC clients
from Unix boxes - and the price is right!

