But it is a convention, yes convention since the beginning,
yes the string handling libraries all assume a null-terminated string,
but you could just as easily use some other means of representing ASCII 
text. Just about the only real assumptions about the machine that C
has a hard time living without is the existence od an 8-bit byte and a
byte-addressable memory system. And even those you can work around. 

K&R did set the convention on null-terminated strings, and it is rational
enough, and very flexible, as something which illustrates a higher-level
construct existing in the onctext of C wihtout being a part of the actual
language. 
Certainly there are times when null-termination on strings can be a pain,
and at those times some small management and explicitly sized buffers
can be maintained to keep track of it, or whatever. you're not _bound_ to
null-terminated text in C!

happy hacking,
gregg

