gleason@mwk.com (Lee K. Gleason) writes in article <oAmQ6esGJFZ8@mwk> dated 27 Aug 98 09:22:02 CST:
>  A department here has a small Alpha server, a 2000 4/275. It has
>three disks in it, an RZ28M-VW and two RZ29B-VA's. They want to add around 
>10 to 20 Gbytes of storage to the system. It's running VMS V6.2.

OK.  "-VW" means wide (16 bit) SCSI; "-VA" means narrow (8 bit) SCSI.  Since
you have -VW working already, you know your hardware at least supports wide
SCSI devices.  

I have an old 2100 with a narrow shelf in it which won't take a -VW.  A lot
of others were shipped with narrow SCSI controller cards but wide shelves. 
These work fine with -VW disks; they just run in narrow mode.  That is
probably what you have.

So pretty much any Storageworks-enclosed disk on the market will work for
you.  Buy -VW in case you want to upgrade the controller later, for faster
throughput.

>  I took a look, and it has some kind of Storage Works "shelf" in it,
>mounted vertically, and has 5 more slots for disks.

The SCSI ID of each disk is determined by its position in the shelf.  The
new ones (top to bottom) are in numerical order -- 01234567.  The one you
have is probably 04152637.

You can't use the bottom slot (7) for a disk.  It's reserved for a power
supply or something.  And if you have a CD and/or tape drive on that same
bus, you won't be able to use those corresponding slots either.

Those shelves have a feature that allows them to be "split" on two different
SCSI busses.  If you have a split shelf and only one controller connected to
it, every other slot will work.  Don't worry; unsplitting them is fairly
straightforward.

I'll let someone else explain about seek time and rotation speed...

