Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> Not to argue with you, but the cost (to CPQ) is not the same. CPQ pays
> the Billionaire a certain number of dollars for each copy of NTWS it
> puts on an Alpha box. With respect to DUNIX and OVMS, CPQ feels that it
> needs to recoup development and deployment expenses for these OSes by
> charging more for them.


That is still not a logical reason why Compaq/Digital takes steps to prevent
VMS from booting
on the low cost workstations. We are talking hardware here. Software licence
costs are what 
goes back to the DUNIX/VMS folks to recup the big investments made instead of
going to Bill Gates.

Take this to the limit: A $1 workstation.   Compaq could sell it for $150.00
with NT running on it
						or sell it for $450.00 with VMS running on it. 

They still make the same money compared to:
						A $50000 workstations. Compaq sells it for $50149.00 with NT and
						               $50449 with VMS running on it.

In both scenarios, Compaq keeps $449.00 for the VMS licence.

But when allowing VMS to boot on cheap workstations, your odds of selling
multiple licences are
much higher. And your odds of slowing down the erosion of VMS are also higher.



