I think it's yet another example of targetted marketing - During the vax
9000 era, dec trying to push into the mainframe market, vms was marketed
as a blue chip os while unix was `snake oil'. With some justification -
at the time unix was crap in terms of security and sys management tools,
while vms was very good, once you had got past the mountain of
verbosity. 

By allowing vms to run on cheap hardware, exclusivness and prestige is
distilled somewhat, or is that over egging it a bit ?. Targetted
marketing is about product positioning, image and price and dec have
always been very good at that. Rememember all the rumours about slugging
the performance of low end vax and pdp so as not to embarass the big
iron ?.

Sun, however are perhaps smarter - For example, Solaris free for home
users will create many converts which will bleed over into corporate
decision making...

Some of the best hardware and the most solid feeling os's- why can't
they wake up...

Just 2 penn'orth...


Chris


