>    until the cows came home   :-(


According to Those In The Know, DEC actually sold 454 of the beasts. Far
fewer than the IDC Fairy Tale known as the Processor Data Book would
tend to indicate.

Couple o' problems with Aquarius, not the least of which is that it was
a couple of years late. So late that the decision was made (by Sultan
Zia) to scuttle the water-cooled version and crank the clock on the
air-cooled Aridus from 20VUP to 30VUP. Therein lies Problem 1: thermal
management.

More importantly, DEC was screwing around with High Density Signal
Carrier (HDSC) technology on the MCUs. Futzing with Unproven Technology
is not a Good Thing to do in the commercial space.

Finally, the CMOS-4 NVAX as used in the VAX 6300 less than a year after
Aquarius' debut blew the 9000 outta the water. Sure, DEC had plans to go
to 150VUP with a GaAs "Centaurus" variant of the 9000, but gallium
arsenide is a bitch to work with. EMP-resistant, yes, but a cast-iron
bitch to fab!

But we all have 20-20 hindsight.

Cheers,

