I know. this is a general algorithm to compute the julian day number from any
gregorain date. if you want the base date be 26-Dec-1909, compute the julian
day number for 26-Dec-1909, and substract this value from the result.

> (b)  They have too much code to fit in the same space as the original
>        algorithm which broke on 05-Feb-2000.

too much code ? the computation 'd/m/y'->'julian' is just :

$ if m.gt.2
$ then
$       m = m - 3
$ else
$       m = m + 9
$       y = y - 1
$ endif
$
$ c = y / 100
$ y = y - 100*c
$
$ j = c*146097/4 + y*1461/4 + (m*153 + 2)/5 + d + 1721119

the "big" part is for 'julian'->'d/m/y' ...
it can easilly be re-worked to be table-driven.

I'm not trying in any way to advocate this algorithm. I've just posted it
because I thouth it could help you to solve your problem.

Pierre.

