If you don't want that monitor, I'll buy it for $5.   We are using the
same monitor in Win95 on a Matrox g100 AGp card that allows us to
program the timing numbers. All we had to buy extraa was that sync on
green adapter from Griffin. the montiro works perfectly after you input
the correct pixel clock, fron and back porches, and sync pulses.    I
don't have the numbers handy, but I recall them from memory to be( and
they might be wrong!):


Resolution....1280H x 1024V
Pix clock....130000mhz
vsyncpulse...9 lines
vfront and back porch 3 lines each
hsyncpulse... 132 pixels
hbackporch... 240 pixels
hfrontporch...96 pixels


syncs at 72hzV and 77khz horizontal frequencies

You can probably get this to work in linux.  After you have a stable
square picture in win95 or linux, remove the cover of the monitor and
adjust the horizontal and vert size pots to push the picture to the edge
of the screen. There is a plastic adjusting screwdriver mounted
alongside the picture tube insdie the monitor. You will have 19.2" of
diagonal viewing area.  Awesome!



