In article <6rplve$ecr$1@einstein.pnm.net>, "Christian J. Bauer" <christian.bauer@passau.netsurf.de> writes:
:I'm a bit confused about connecting a Keyboard and the Mouse to my
:VAXStation 3100/38 SPX. The System Manual states that in "some"
:configurations (GPX?) it is not allowed (recommended?) to connect the
:Keyboard/Mouse to the System thru the Monitor cable.
:
:Up to now, I always connected the Keyboard and the Mouse directly to the
:system, but due to space reasons, I want to give it a try thru the monitor
:cable.
:
:Is it possible/recommended? 

  It is not recommended.

  If the configuration does not work, then the keyboard and/or mouse will
  start to behave erratically (and that comment is not intended to serve
  as a straight line) and/or the video display will become erratic.

:Will it harm the system ?

  If you are certain you have the correct cables for your current system
  hardware configuration, then equipment damage is unlikely.

  I do remember having various problems video-related using video cables
  that included the keyboard and mouse signals...  I'd tend to avoid this
  configuration...

  Remember to power down the entire system before plugging or unplugging...

