The above algorithms seem just fine.  But, unfortunately, although they
are obviously great for the problem they solve, I cannot use either for
the following 2 reasons:
(a) The base year is not 26-Dec-1909 and the application programs
      must keep compatible with the existing data base,  and
(b)  They have too much code to fit in the same space as the original
       algorithm which broke on 05-Feb-2000.
In addition, as a minor aspect, the algorithm which I suggested in my post
is likely to be a fair bit faster and use less memory.  While it would need
a bit of tweaking to successfully cross 2000 from 1582, as well as not
having leap years for the centuries (I think I just repeated myself), the
use of a table driven method seems to be able to yield a faster answer.
If anyone would like a solution starting with 15-Oct-1582 to the
post I gave on 19-Aug-1998  22:05:19-04:00, I will try and re-work
my calculation.  The key point is that in my calculation, there are many
fewer divisions and multiplication.  Also, all the arithmetic can be
carried out in 16 bits except for the last step; thus only 2 more
instructions would be required to have a 32 bit result starting on
15-Oct-1582, plus the fudge factor because the centuries are
not leap years (complicated because 2000 is a leap year)
something like:   (  15  -  Y / 100  )  +  (  Y / 1000 - 1 )  which
becomes:  Y  /  1000  -  Y  /  100  + 14
Obviously, the table of days,  DT,  would change as well.
Just thinking on paper how my algorithm would change.

I also have an algorithm for the conversion of the julian date
to the M/D/Y.  This algorithm is table driven and requires a
loop to determine the month via repeated comparisons.
It was just finished this morning and I haven't yet converted
it to assembler, but only 1 divide is required and one
multiply (and a shift for a divide by 4 for leap days).  The
final loop to determine the month should require only 3
instructions a maximum of 12 tries.  Since the only
arithmetic operation in the loop is a compare, the loop
should operate more quickly than a divide instruction,
at least on a PDP-11 since the average number of instructions
that will be executed in the loop is only 18.  Note that I
