[snip]
>I presume that 1600, 1700, 1800 and 1900 were all non-leap years.
>And from the algorithm, it would seem that every future century is also a
>non-leap year except when it is a millennium.  Just how far does that rule
>hold in the future and might the earth's rotation be slowing enough such
>that in the future, some millennium will also no longer be a leap year?
Jerome,
The Gregorian leap year rule is every year evenly divisible by four,
except if evenly divisible by 100, unless also evenly divisible by 400:
so 2000 is a leap year, 1900 and 2100 are not!
Hopefully we will have adopted a more rational calendar before the next
leap century (2400) comes around.
>The best information I have here is the start date of the Gregorian Calendar!!!
The Gregorian calendar was adopted by different countries at different
times, from the date above, when it was adopted by European Catholic
countries, up to this century, when it was adopted by the USSR, Turkey,
Japan and other Asian countries as the official business calendar. 
It was not adopted by Great Britain and its colonies (the U.S. and most
of the English speaking world) until September 1752, so 1700 *was* a
leap year, in our (Julian) epoch, but 1800 was not, in the new
(Gregorian) epoch.
