In article <6r1isk$etm$1@flex.london.pipex.net>, Roger Golesworthy wrote:
>
>I am trying to track down information on a DEC product which I believe is
>still supported. It seems to go under the name of DECadvantage, specifically
>I am interested in the X.25 (on NWP) .

When DEC first got into selling/supporting SCO Unix, a couple of engineering
teams were set up to build SMP PC's (the ApplicationDEC 433MP for example) and
also to qualify SCO on the PC's we shipped at the time.  We also brought a
division from Philips and a West German computer company called Kienzle (owned
by Mannesman) who sold UNIX solutions into Legal markets.  The engineering
teams from these two companies merged to form a new group in Appledorn (Holland)
and it was this new group that came up with the DECadvantage product set. X.25
was just one part of it.  Another part was based on Wollangong TCP, there was
a kernel tuning/performance tool and others I think too, but I forget.

Years later, when DEC pulled away from reselling SCO, the SCO focused
engineering groups were disolved and Philips/Kienzle outfit got sold off.
I can't remember if it was a management buy out.  Anyhow, they changed their
name and now they're called Axxia.  I've no idea if they took the DECadvantage
product with them, or if it remained the property of Digital.  You might want
to ask them that question yourself.   Their contact details are:

