From: Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com>
Subject: Re: Y2K Conversion of Date to Internal Format
Date: 1998/08/17
Message-ID: <35D8AD45.458E8C95@idirect.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 382104286
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
X-Trace: 17 Aug 1998 22:23:53 GMT, ts6-23t-18.idirect.com
Organization: "Usenet User"
MIME-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

I am in the process of converting some application
programs which have been written in FORTRAN.
The complete package uses an internal DATE value
which starts on 26-Dec-1909 as 0 and increases for
each day.  The algorithm which is broken is:
DATE = CONVRT ( MONTH, DAY, YEAR )
MONTH = MONTH - 2
IF ( MONTH .LE. 0 ) MONTH = MONTH + 12; YEAR = YEAR - 1
Q = YEAR / 100
R =  YEAR - Q * 100
DATE = ( YEAR - 1910 ) * 365   +   Q / 4   +   R / 4   +
                                   ( MONTH + 1 ) * 28   +   ( 26 * MONTH - 2 ) / 10
where all variables are 16 bit integers.  Obviously the value overflows and
becomes negative on 13-Sep-1999 which is exactly what is wanted.

I have taken a few liberties with the FORTRAN code since I wanted
to make the algorithm clear.

The problem is that the algorithm BREAKS on 01-Mar-2000 at which
point the DATE value reverts to the same value as for 06-Feb-2000
and 24 duplicates are created.  There is also a routine which works in
the opposite direction which just ignores the first set of duplicate days
from 06-Feb-2000 to 29-Feb-2000.

(A)  Is anyone familiar with an algorithm which works to convert
        to the internal format and in the reverse direction?
(B)  The size is important since I MUST patch the application
        programs, NOT compile and link.

I suspect that someone thought we should revert to the
old Julian Calendar as opposed to the current Gregorian
calendar when those 11? days were lost and give everyone a
change to live 24 days a second time.   I though a bit of
humour might make the question a bit more interesting?

I have added this portion of my request and modified the
paragraph above in order to clarify things.  First, although
the algorithm will obviously be of a general nature, the actual
implementation will be on a PDP-11 running a program
under TSX-PLUS.  The final language used to do the
implementation will be coding in machine language since
the changes will be PATCHES to an existing program.
I do intend to use assembler (or MACRO) to do some
of the testing.  SO, no VMS (nor for that matter any other
operating system dependent interactions) are allowed.
This is, as expressed above, the necessity for a substitute
algorithm for the current on which breaks on 06-Feb-2000.
The inputs are:  MONTH,   DAY,   YEAR
The output is:  Number of days since 26-Dec-1909.
Note that on 12-Sep-1999, this value is 32767.  On the
next day, the value overflows (from a technical point of
view) and becomes 32768.  That value does not exist as
far as FORTRAN  IV  is concerned since integers range
from -32768 to +32767.  That does not mean that the
correct UNSIGNED value cannot be returned from
a program written in machine language (MACRO)
and used for subsequent calculations (although it is
recognized that in some cases the FORTRAN generated
instructions which use signed branches must be changed
to unsigned branches - already done once when an
expiry date of 31-Dec-1999 was required).

Comments on the algorithm, although interesting, are
not as appreciated as would a new algorithm which
works.  Incidentally, the reverse algorithm must also
be produced since it is absolutely necessary to
convert from the internal format to M/D/Y.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict
Year 2000 Solutions for Legacy RT-11 Applications
(Sources not always required)




