I'm sure Jerome has solved this by now, but ...

Rob Brown <robbrown@shaw.wave.ca> wrote in article
<01bdcac2$35061860$7df84018@fsys.wave.shaw.ca>...
> 
> 
> Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com> wrote in article
> <35D8AD45.458E8C95@idirect.com>...
> > DATE = CONVRT ( MONTH, DAY, YEAR )
> > MONTH = MONTH - 2
> > IF ( MONTH .LE. 0 ) MONTH = MONTH + 12; YEAR = YEAR - 1
> > Q = YEAR / 100
> > R =  YEAR - Q * 100
> > DATE = ( YEAR - 1910 ) * 365   +   Q / 4   +   R / 4   +
> >                                    ( MONTH + 1 ) * 28   +   ( 26 *
> >                                    MONTH - 2 ) / 10

>            The problem obviously has something to do with the 1999 ->
> 2000 transition, but I can't see what it is right now.

As Kelvin Smith observed in another article, the problem is that when
the year changes from 1999 to 2000, the Q/4 term contributes one more
day, but the R/4 term contributes 24 less.

The solution is to get rid of Q and R and just use a term containing
YEAR/4.  You don't need to worry about the fact that 2100 is not a leap
year.  Jerome stated he was using 16 bit integer arithmetic, which
overflows May 31, 2089.

So the corrected algorithm is:

  MONTH = MONTH - 2
  IF (MONTH .LE. 0) THEN
    MONTH = MONTH + 12
    YEAR = YEAR - 1
  ENDIF
  DATE = (YEAR-1910)*365      ! 365 days per year
       + (YEAR-1910+2)/4      ! leap day every four years
       + (MONTH+1)*28         ! amazing days per month calculation
       + (26*MONTH-2)/10
       + DAY
       + 6                    ! magic number to make it all
                              ! come out right, 
                              ! but coincidentally the number of days
                              ! from Dec 26 to Jan 1.

You're welcome.

- Rob


