Jerome Fine replies:

Thank you very much.  To all those individuals who had a hand
in the above solution plus, a very helpful explanation as to why
the "amazing days per month calculation" works (see his next post
by Rob Brown of 1 second later).  In addition, you are correct
in that I also solved the problem, and in a different manner.
It is debatable as to which solution is better or more elegant,
but if I may present my solution, the reader can be his own
judge.  Note that even though my final result was coded
for a  FORTRAN  IV  environment where the integers are
all signed 16 bits words in a PDP-11 environment (the
code was done in machine language since I may only patch the
original  SAV  file), the essence is in this  FORTRAN  77
subroutine.  I would expect that coding it in  FORTRAN  IV
would yield the same result, but it would not be simple to
print it out when  IDENC  becomes 32768 on 13-Sep-1999.

FUNCTION  INTEGER*4  IDENC  (  MONTH,  DAY,  YEAR )

INTEGER*2  MONTH,  DAY,  YEAR,  M,  Y,  YLEAP,  DT ( 12 )
INTEGER*4  YLONG
DATA  DT  / 64, 95, 125, 156, 186, 217, 248, 278, 309, 339, 370, 401 /

M  =  MONTH  -  2
Y  =   YEAR
IF  (  M  .LE.  0 )  THEN
      M  =  M  +  12
      Y   =  Y  -  1
      ENDIF
YLONG  =  Y
YLONG  =  YLONG  *  365
YLEAP   =   (  Y  -  1908 )  /  4

IDENC  =  YLONG  +  YLEAP  +  DT ( M )  + DAY

END

Notice that this algorithm starts out using the same transformation
of placing January and February at the end of the year along with
the leap day when it is present.  This makes it very easy to account
for leap days in a manner which affects the rest of the calculation
as little as possible.  Thus, the only significant difference in the
method is the use of a table to determine the number of days
up the the start of the given month.  In principle, this method
is faster, but takes somewhat more overall code and data.
Fortunately, this correct algorithm, as well as the one above,
both fit into the same code space as the original incorrect
algorithm. I am referring the to assembly language versions.
I did not take the trouble to code the other method, but I
am assuming that it would also fit in less code space since
the Q and R fudge factors have been combined and the
calculation should take fewer words.  What I can't figure
out is why Zeller did not also use a table driven method
as I did since in principle it is faster, uses less total memory
(than his incorrect procedure) and is much easier to
understand.

As stated, I believe the algorithm can be easily extended
both backward and forward by hundreds of years.  What
the leap year situation is like after 2100 (which we all agree
will not be a leap year), I have no idea.  However, if the
total number of days covered by the complete range
exceeds 65535 days in total, then a 32 bit result would
be required, which the above example already provides.
In assembler, the code would be almost identical since
the PDP-11 already produces a 32 bit output for the
multiply instruction.   A few minor additions to the
rest of the calculation (in PDP-11, a few Adc instructions)
and the result is complete.

One other addition to this subroutine is the reverse:

SUBROUTINE  IDDEC  (  DATE,  MONTH,  DAY,  YEAR )

If anyone would like the algorithm for this calculation, please
send an e-mail.  This post is already too long.
If enough replies are received, I will post IDDEC in a few days
for all to see.

Again, the interest is very much appreciated

Sincerely yours,

