Dan,

For other people who find themselves in the same quandry, the Intel
83mz Pentium OverDrive is incompatible with many (most?) 486
motherboards, because Intel decided to add a write-back cache feature
to the CPU after most of the 486 boards were designed and
manufactured.  Intel refers to an "interposer" card in their web site,
but I doubt you'd be able to find one.  The interposer is a CPU-sized
card that accepts the Pentium OverDrive, and plugs into Socket 3.  Its
sole purpose is to force the internal cache to write-thru mode,
compatible with most any 486 board.

Best bet is to upgrade a DEC pc XL to a true Pentium-based system.
This involves replacing the 486 main logic board and processor card.
The DEC PowerGrade kits for DECpc XL systems do exactly this.

Now for the bald advertising...

I probably have most of the unsold PowerGrade kits on the planet,
having bought out a bunch of DEC surplus.  My prices for DEC pc XL
PowerGrade kits.  I can guarantee they will be technically correct,
but if you're a purist, I may run out of the newer plastic front
panels that say "Celebris XL5133" or whatever.

DECpc XL to Celebris XL 5133, 133 MHz Pentium $175
DECpc XL to Celebris XL 5150, 150 MHz Pentium $195
DECpc XL to Celebris XL 5166, 166 MHz Pentium $210
DECpc XL to Celebris XL with your own CPU and heat sink $125   

I don't have any Pentium 200 chips on hand, and these now sell for
$130 to $150 each when you can find them.

I may also be able to scrape together parts to do a Celebris XL
Pentium Pro upgrade or two, one of the models 6150, 6166, or 6200, but
that will have to wait a week or two.

... Ben Myers

