>> Chris Ulrich wrote:
>>> transistors.  Even after the 21264 is shrunk to .25 micron,
>>> it will still use twice the transistors and likely be *much*
>>> more expensive than a comparable xeon or related Ppro core
>>> intel CPU.

>Most transistors are used for I & D caches (2x64 Kb of cache eats a lot of
>millions Cmos transistors). 15 millions is near the edge with a 0.35 process,
>but quite good for a 0.25 process. 

Exactly.  Most of those transistors are caches, as LaSalle points out.
This make the 21264 have a bigger die, but is that so bad?  It's possible
to design caches/memory arrays to be defect-tolerant.  I don't know if
DEC has done this, but it'd sure improve their yields if they added
some extra memory to the 128k, to use as a backup:  if the CPU detects
some bad memory, substitute the extra memory in that place.

That can do a lot to ease the manufacturabilty of the machines, if 
some defects in the cache can be repaired.

PeterM



