  From the spec web page:
Compaq Computer Digital Alpha server 8400 6/575:
	(575mhz 21264, 64+64cache, 4mb L2 cache, 512mb ram, DUNIX)
	hardware available Nov-98
		CINT95:		30.3
		  (CINT_BASE):	26.0
		CFP95:		47.7
		  (CFP95_BASE):	40.9
Intel Corporation Intel MS440GX Motherboard
	(400mhz xeon, 16+16cache, 1mb L2 cache, 128mb ram, NT)
	hardware available Jul-98
		CINT95:		16.5
		  (CINT_BASE):	16.5
		CFP95:		13.7
		  (CFP95_BASE):	12.4

  This is a $250,000 computer that may not ship for at least 3
months compared to a $5,000 computer you can buy at the store
down the street.  The raw speed is impressive, but the cost of
the machine that achieved it is shocking.
  Single CPU systems (low cost workstation type systems) will
probably get better SPEC numbers because they can have faster
memory subsystems, but how long will it take for CPQ to make
enough 21264 cpus to saturate the demand in the $100,000+
segment of the market?  How long after that will it take for
them to saturate the $20,000 workstation market?  Only after
they've made so many 21264 CPUs that they can't sell them all
in $20,000+ systems will they actually start competing with 
Intel.  I suspect it will be years before the 21264 displaces
the 21164(shrunk) in the "el cheapo" market that intel 
dominates.
  Lastly, from what I've read, the 21264, is made with a .35
micron feature size and 15 million transistors.  The Xeon 
is made with .25 micron feature size and has 7.5 million 
transistors.  Even after the 21264 is shrunk to .25 micron,
it will still use twice the transistors and likely be *much*
more expensive than a comparable xeon or related Ppro core
intel CPU.
  I expect the 21164 will be the mainstream alpha for at least
another 18 months.
chris
