As another poster mentioned, you can use Netscape Mail on OpenVMS
to do this.

I'm going to make an assumption that you recieve you mail on OpenVMS
and you, from time to time, get attachments that VMSMAIL barfs on?

If that is the case, and you have a POP3 server on your VMS system, 
you can set things up to be able to have a MIME-capabile mail utility
read files correctly.

In my case 95% of the mail I get is "normal" good 'ol text messages, 
things like "build completed"  or "test run complete" things like
that - I don't need to decode these.

I have UCX's SMTP server configured to allow attachments to be 
decoded.  When I do get an attachment, I move the mail message back
into my NEWMAIL folder, bring up Netscape Mail and do a 
GET MESSAGES, with Netscape mails' pop3 server pointing at 
UCX's POP3 server running on my OpenVMS system.  Poof I'm done.  

Its an extra step but I spend way too much time with non-attachment
type files to justify moving away from VAXMail.

