Several of the NetBSD developers with 233MHz systems have had the same
problem.  I suspect it has more to do with the typical duty cycle of
the machines; people were using them, running NetBSD, as small webservers,
PPP routers, NAT boxes, development machines, etc.  They probably weren't
ever off, and a fair amount of the time they were probably actually being
used.  The one that I had running NT first exhibited the signs when I left
it on, running the optimizer from the DEC binary translator for a couple
of weeks -- first I installed every piece of software I could find around
the office, used them all for a bit, then told it to go to work.  After
about a week of work, it started freezing at random.

Maybe only some of the Multias made had problem parts coming out of the
factory.  At this point, who would ever know?

FWIW I consider Multias to be pretty much disposable hardware -- cute,
useful, but disposable -- and I didn't care enough to bug the guy I
bought mine from.  I expect many others feel likewise.

Now if my Shark (DEC-built NetBSD/arm32 NC) just had the Multia's SCSI
and PCMCIA slots... sigh.  But then again, it was designed to cost,
