John F Carr wrote:

> When DEC instroduced a revolutionary new architecture, they didn't 
> ship their first Alpha chip.

The first Alpha chip, EV3, contained no floating point.  It was never
intended to be product, however, it was very useful for O.S. and
compiler debug.  The EV3 was shrunk down to CMOS4 which allowed enough
room to add back the floating point unit and eventually became known as
the Alpha 21064 product.

We also made extensive use of a software emulator for Alpha that ran on
a VAX system.  We were able to boot the entire Alpha OpenVMS operating
system on the Alpha instruction emulator.  It took about an hour runtime
on a VAX 8800!  When the EV3 was finally available, it only took us
about a week to debug the remaining code on the actual hardware to get
to the login prompt.

...but then, I guess, I'm giving away all of our secrets!

I'm sure the Intel folks have there own ideas.

 
