Sorry, Can't agree. The real killer was DEC's appointment of Palmer as
CEO. Without him, they had a chance. By deliberately managing it into
the ground he hoped no-one would see he wasn't up to the job of turning
around DEC's fortunes. He instead decided to improve his own at DEC's
(and the industry's expense). With Palmer at the healm DEC had no
chance.

They might have reversed out of their woes if they's had a marketing
department that understood the environment in which the company was now
working. Reliability became much less important - Flashy became the key
to the money box. And pricing was plain stupid - so many boxes prices
not to sell. There just weren't enough consumers in the biggest market
sector who were still so "unprice-conscious" and DEC just could see it.
It used to break my heart to watch.

Courting the media just wasn't done right. I remember a mag called "DEC
Computing" that was so anti-DEC people binned it in case their boss read
it and thought they'd made bad decisions in buying DEC.

If any other company had come up with VMS clusters or Galaxy, even
people in IBM would have known about it. Because the good news was left
to DEC's marketing department, the message was lost - so much so that a
UK journalist who recently discovered clusters didn't know DEC had
anything to do with the idea.

DEC had a chance with cheap alphas - if they'd sold them at a price that
made them the natural choice on the desktop, they could have turned
things around but, again, their marketing let them down. They threw all
their effort into selling the competition's products instead of making
their own product more attractive.

When they wanted to shed a few staff, they didn't make any managerial
decisions about who they would need in the future organisation, nope -
nothing that clever - they let the individuals decide for themselves.
Those who knew they could sell their skills elsewhere, and had no other
reason for staying, went. Those who could see the problems  in DEC's
management and had become frustrated and hoarse pleading to the deaf
ears also left. The people who left were the one's needed to keep - and
would have if they'd had competent managers. (In a survey I did for DEC
in 1977 - the majority feeling, even at senior management level, was
that the companies greatest strength was its products and its greatest
weakness was it managment). I worked for DEC again in the UK in the
mid-80s and this had become so obvious it wasn't even worth a formal
survey.

The bit that surprises me most is the decision to do away with DEC to
the point of getting rid of it as a brand. I can't see the business
sense in that at all - Compaq said it wanted to buy DEC's corporate
customer base but immediately showed that it can not be trusted as a
business partner. Demolishing DEC completely is not a decision guided by
business prudence. The real story will probably not come out for a long
time yet.

With all DEC's faults, the IT industry is definitely poorer for its
departure.

