On Thu, 6 Aug 1998 00:39:44 +0100, Jean-Marc Troit <troit@globalnet.co.uk>
wrote:
>I've tried connecting a VT320 terminal on the Vax MMJ port but nothing
>appeared. I made the cable myself trying every possibility I could think of
>as well as configuring the VT320 in many different modes, all whithout any
>success....

Oog. I can't speak directly to your VT320, but I've used both a -220 and a
-420 on my 4000/300.

The machine's console port is the one inside the front door, just to the
right of the diagnostic display. It's a standard MMJ terminal connection, so
a standard MMJ cable will work just fine; plug the cable directly into that
port on the machine, and directly into the back of your VT320 (if it has an
MMJ port) or via an MMJ-RS232 adapter if not. (Aside: a standard MMJ cable
will, when stretched out flat, have the pins on the connectors at each end
facing the same way. The inherent crossover this does is what lets you plug
two terminals - and, for this purpose, the VAX is a terminal - together.)

The switches in the top center of the CPU panel on the VAX should be set as
follows: the top three-position switch should be set to the top (fully
clockwise) position: this tells the machine to always ask for the language
at startup; the middle one (the white rotary switch) should, until you are
sure everything works, be set to 5, for 9600 baud; the bottom of the three,
a slide swith, should be placed in the up position, which tells the machine
to start up at the >>> prompt. At this point, set your terminal to 9600
baud, no parity, 8 data bits, one stop bit, and data leads only. When you
power up the machine, you should get a menu asking what language you want,
and then a countdown, stopping eventually at the >>> prompt.

If you don't see any of this, one other thing you can check is the
diagnostic display. Unlike other VAXen, this won't count straight down from
F to 3, but it will change repeatedly, eventually stopping at 3 when you get
the >>> prompt.

Good luck...



