David Hagood  <wowbagger@sktc.net> wrote:
>I just picked up an old Multia 166 for $125, and would like to add a bit
>more hard drive space to it (360M is a little thin for a usable Linux
>install) and would like to use the 2.5" IDE drives I have laying around.
>I tried connecting to the IDE port in the unit, but the system doesn't
>seem to be able to find which interrupt the IDE uses, which locks things
>up nicely. Anyone have any suggestions?

These machines run NetBSD fine, including X11.  You can get a NetBSD install,
except for X, onto the 360MB drive if you're careful.  Then you can put
X and whatever else you want to on an IDE drive -- unfortunately the Multia
firmware can't boot from IDE, so you need to keep the SCSI drive to boot from.

NetBSD-current can use the IDE on the Multia.  A recent NetBSD-current
snapshot is available at ftp://ftp.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/arch/alpha/snapshot.

Be aware that without a moderately involved hardware fix (desoldering and
replacing at least one IC and possibly several resistor packs on the bottom
of the unit, all surface-mount) Multias tend to fail over time, displaying
a behaviour where they lock up suddenly, or memory data is corrupted.  This
happens, unsurprisingly enough, no matter what OS you run on them.  The
thing is, by now it's a safe bet that _most_ Multias have started exhibiting
the symptoms... so you may want to have that soldering iron handy.

