peter@baileynm.com wrote:

> In article <handleym-0708982247180001@macip3-170.apple.com>,
> Maynard Handley <handleym@ricochet.net> wrote:
> >> The logic behind Intel's solution is that copper is far less plentiful in
> >> the world than aluminium, according to a chip analyst. Aluminium is
> >> extracted from a mineral called bauxite while copper needs a variety of
> >> complicated chemicalprocesses to turn it into the pure metal. This
> >> explains why ladders, and other familiar household items such as kettles,
> >> are more likely to be fashioned from aluminium than copper, the analyst
> >> said.
>
> >Call me crazy, but I find it really hard to believe that the raw cost of
> >the copper in a CPU is a significant factor in the CPU price.
> >Does this "analyst" have a point, or is (s)he just plain loony?
>
> I thought that was rather odd myself. Hell, given the amount of raw metal
> in one of those things you could use gold or platinum and not count the
> cost.

 The real reason is that copper contaminates the thin layers of silicon
metal that are applied to the surface of the wafer during manufacturing.
A barrier must be applied to prevent copper contamination.
This is not necessary with aluminum. If it were possible, silver would be
used. Silver is a better conductor than copper.
The raw cost of the metal is not significant.




