Well is that is the type of "logic" that Intel is using then I'd sell my
Intel shares (if I had any) ASAP!  This is pure FUD!!!

> > the world than aluminium, according to a chip analyst. Aluminium is
> > extracted from a mineral called bauxite while copper needs a variety 
It is true that Al is far more common than Cu.  It exists in all clays in
combination with Si an O and makes up a significant proportion of the Earth's
crust (6% or there abouts comes to mind!).  However, no one in the right mind
tries to separate this Al+Si+O party.  It's simply TOO expensive in energy
terms.

of
> > complicated chemicalprocesses to turn it into the pure metal. This
> > explains why ladders, and other familiar household items such as kettles,
> > are more likely to be fashioned from aluminium than copper, the analyst
> > said.

The process to extract Cu from it's ores had been known for 3,000 to 4,000
years so there is little secret there.  The chemical steps to produce pure
(or near pure) Al from Bauxite (Al+O combination, no Si) are certainly more
esoteric requiring the use of Flurine to replace the O and then PRODIGOUS
amount of electricity passed through pure carbon rods to separate the Al from
the Fl.  Copper also uses electrolysis but is not quite so hungry.  The cost
of AL .v. Cu is a scarcity+processing cost thing.

> Call me crazy, but I find it really hard to believe that the raw cost of
> the copper in a CPU is a significant factor in the CPU price.
> Does this "analyst" have a point, or is (s)he just plain loony?

I don't think the raw cost has anything to do with it.  Al is cheaper than Cu
but it's main advantage is lower melting/vapour point than Cu so it is easier
to deposit. It has been used for electricity conductors in power transmission
even though it has a higher resistance than Cu because of it's relative
cheapness with adequate conduction properties.

Intel will start using Cu it when it has the technology to do so, but I
imagine that IBM will be breathing down their necks looking for patent
violations!!!

> Maynard
