In article <35C7A9C0.267@ibm.net>, Pat Gunn  <pgunn01@ibm.net> wrote:
>#define CHAR1 '\r' /* This is the one I'm not sure about.
[...]
>while(mychar = fgetc(ifile))
>	{
>	if(mychar == EOF)
>		break;
>	if(mychar == 'CHAR1')
>		continue; /* Diddle with logic here to go the other way */
>	fputc(ofile,mychar);
>	}

Careful, you've fallen victim to the common misconception (unfortunately
shared by C-Kermit) that converting from a regular ASCII file to a UNIX text
file requires removing *all* CRs, whereas in fact you only want to remove
CRs that immediately preceed LFs.  This program will screw up files that
use CR for overprinting.

I've got a couple of quicky DOS programs to do the conversion correctly at
http://www.dbit.com/pub/ibmpc/util/addcr.com and .../remcr.com, unfortunately
they're filters so they're a bit cumbersome to use, but anyway the above
program would work if you searched for a CR/LF sequence and replaced *that*
with just LF -- so when you see a CR, set a flag instead of writing (or not
writing) anything out, and then next time through the loop, check the flag
and write the CR out after all if the current char is not LF (then clear
the flag, either way).  Ah, also get rid of the single quotes around CHAR1.

John Wilson
D Bit



