> Hong Kong, all the inspection stamps have HK in them...  Output caps all 

  Ok. DEC has used some marginal vendors in the past. In particular, Astec
(who we used to call "spazz-tech", but they've gotten better) and the outfit
(I forget there name) who made the LEP (external TK50, RD5x) power supply.

> look fine, but one near the transformer (16v, 470uF) is a bit bulged; I'll
> try to locate a replacement.  The switching transistor (assuming I'm looking
> at the right piece; IRFPE30 4T30 8947, screwed to a heat sink, larger than
> a TO-220 type) looks perfect FWIW.

  It may not show any external damage but still be bad.

>      Thanks for the info; at least there's a small chance of fixing this
> inexpensively if I can't find a vendor with a cheap replacement.

  Try doing a web search for something like "switching power supply theory"
or "switching power supply troubleshooting". As I recall, there are a couple
of good sites. Just remember - these things *bite*!. Large ones can maintain
lethal charges for long periods of time (an 11/44 supply with the bleeder
resistors removed can stay over 300 volts for over half an hour. This is
likely a much smaller unit (I've never seen one) but live troubleshooting
can still be dangerous.

  You may want to look in a Mouser or Newark catalog for a supply with sim-
ilar ratings and form factor if you can't get this one fixed or find an exact
replacement. Generic parts are likely a lot less expensive, and since you
have the bad supply, you should have all the necessary connectors to adapt
a generic unit.

