>"swapping PALCode" (with a milo I got for this machine specifically).
>Currently it has ARC firmware. I originally wanted to switch to SRM
>firmware, as it looked more like what I'm used to from my experiences
>with NeXT and SUN hardware, but all the things I've read have suggested
>staying with ARC if I'm going to be running a non-OSF OS on it. I wish

Well, that's what you get for reading some random web page in this
Linux-overrun world.  If you want to run NetBSD, you'll need to use SRM.
It's easy enough to switch the firmware to SRM.


>I could put milo in the flashrom if it were possible, but it looks like
>this system doesn't support that (again from the documentation avail on
>the web)

(and why bother?) :-)

>I've borrowed a Matrox Millenium and monitor from another of my systems
>and sort of successfully did a net install of RedHat, but
>I don't like RedHat and some things were broken beyond how much
>Redhat normally is broken (i.e. man gets a SIGSEGV with normal usage on
>this box, I couldn't mount a FAT partition I made to put milo and
>loadlin on, etc)

This is atypical for Linux?

>Anyhow, putting the card for this box back in and hooking it back up to
>its monitor causes it to hang at the part above at the MILO/video
>interation
>problem. I'm pretty sure the card works in itself -- I can get around
>all
>the ARC menus and have upgraded the firmware and stuff.
>
>So, I guess I'm asking for advice on how to do a net install of
>[Open/Net]bsd, Stampede Linux, or Debian Linux on this box, handling
>firmware issues and setting it up to boot automatically using
>the hardware that's in there now.

You can easily enough install NetBSD from floppy; once you have the
floppy booted it's pretty simple to do the rest of the install from the
network.  The NetBSD/alpha web pages which you'll find on
http://www.netbsd.org/ are fairly good, and there's a mailing list,
port-alpha, whose subscribers should be able to help you with any specific
questions.

These machines seem to run NetBSD pretty well.  Support for the PCMCIA
slots doesn't quite work yet, but should imminently.  An X server for
the 8MB video may take a bit longer.

>I do have the following specific questions:
>	1) Does the ZLXp-E2 have VGA text modes? If not, does
>		the firmware emulate this? Does this even
>		theoretically work in the console modes of
>		the OS's I've mentioned?

IIRC, it may have a VGA chip on it.  It doesn't really matter; NetBSD
knows how to use a TGA as a console already.  I think you'll need
NetBSD-current (you'll find a recent snapshot on ftp.netbsd.org) to
use the ZLXp-E2, rather than 1.3.2.

>	2) Why shouldn't I use the SRM console?

No reason.

>	3) Why can't I zap milo into the prom of this box?

Because then you'd become a mad evangelizing Linux drone, continually
proselytizing on behalf of an operating system that runs on about
1/2 the Alpha systems that NetBSD does. ;-) ;-) ;-)

>	4) Are there equivilants of Stop-A (SUN) or
>		shift-shift-tilde (NeXT) on this box?

With SRM, I think you can get the reset button to drop you to the prompt;
this needs kernel support.  It's there on the other reset-button-full Alpha
machines I've run NetBSD on.

If you build the DDB debugger into your NetBSD kernel, control-alt-esc
will drop you into DDB, from which you may continue at your leisure.

>Although I know the ZLXp-E2 lacks support under XFree, I've heard
>about closed-source X servers that I can use until/unless support
>for that hardware is avaiable. Anyone know for sure where I can
>snag those?

I don't.  There's an X server for the 2MB TGA cards (and the Multia video)
in the NetBSD xsrc tree; has been for a long time.  IIRC the 8MB ZLXp-E2
isn't tremendously different and I presume someone will get it going
eventually.  Hey, it could be you! :-)

>Replies by email would be appreciated. Failing that, a reply to
>the newsgroups would be ok.
>
>BTW -- Please don't turn this into an OS vs OS debate. I can make
>any of those OS's livable for me, and would be roughly equally
>happy to get any of them working on this system w/ this hardware.

In case you hadn't noticed, I'm a Berkeley bigot.  But at least I try to
be helpful. 8-)

