  only posted replies to a couple of the core newsgroups, and I've set
  all followups to this message to the comp.sys.dec newsgroup.

  I've not heard of an AlphaStation 500/1000 -- I'll assume the system in
  question is one of the existing AlphaStation 500 series systems.  I will
  further assume that you have the "console" environment variable set to
  "serial", and that you are using the SRM console.

  Various of the AlphaStations, such as the AlphaStation 255, have a front
  panel button that can be set to "reset" or to "halt", based on a switch
  inside the chassis.  (I haven't looked at the innards of an AlphaStation
  500 series box.)

  A <break> is a serial line framing error, so there are a variety of ways
  to generate one -- deliberately generating serial line data at the wrong
  baud rate is one way.  Power-cycling certain older terminals was another
  good way to generate a serial line framing error (and thus halting the
  system when the terminal in question was the console).  With most standard
  serial-line console terminals, the F5 key will generate a <break>.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
