In article <6pu12l$5v$1@netra.msu.montana.edu>, tarka@msu.oscs.montana.edu (Mark Tarka) writes:
[snip]
>
|>I've got all the boards out and itemized.  It's time to start on the
|>upper compartment.
|>
|>Anyone with any "gotchas" to relate (e.g. static sensitive parts...)
|>or tips on hammering this baby back into existence?

The BA213 is pretty straightforward. Undo some screws (four?) and the grill
comes off. Each drive (up to three?) will be mounted on rails which in turn
screw in via two obvious screws into matching rails attached to the cabinet
(these latter rails will most likely only be present in those "bays" that
had a drive mounted. The TK50/TK70 will be similarly mounted in the RH bay.
There will be various cabling for the drives and for the TK (and a few
ordinary power leads).

All pretty straightforward and nothing particularly static-sensitive.

If the box has been dropped as badly as it sounds (and it sounds like someone
has taken a sledgehammer to it) then I would be surprised if any of the
drives have survived. I don't hit my boards with a hammer, so I cannot comment
on those. If the PSUs check out (and both do need to work for the box
to work) then try just the CPU on its own. It won't pass self-test without
memory but if it talks to you at all it (and the backplane) is probably OK.
Then add the memory and check that self-test passes. Then check out whatever
remaining boards you have individually.

|>Then, I'd probably install what may be a KA640 cpu (SN
|>70-25775-01?).  Do I need to know any info about the cpu...special
|>numbers that I'd need to pass to Compaq's Digital people related to
|>licensing and getting paper copies of any important stuff?
|>

There may well be a serial number on your CPU but the number you quote is
just a part number. Look for something like AA12345BB1 (i.e. roughly that
combination of letters and numbers). I don't know if the licence can be
tracked down from that. Probably all you need to get the right replacement is
the module number M7624-xx where xx tells you whether it was a timesharing
or server CPU. Alternatively the part number(s) on the EPROM(S) will tell
you that (look for 23-11111-00).

Antonio

