    	Zane Healy <"healyzh "@ ix . netcom . com> writes:
> I just ordered one of the Alphastation 200 4/233 systems from
> www.onsale.com and am wondering what it will cost to run OpenVMS 
> on it.  I've spent the last couple hours pouring through DejaNews 
> and the Digital web sites.
> 
> It appears that I'll need the following:
> 
> Media Kit
> Base License
> Concurrent User License (single user)
> 
> My question is, will I need all these, what is the best way to get 
> this and the cheapest.  It's for personal use as I like running VMS 
> at home (already have a VS3100/30 running VMS6.1 on a hobbiest 
> license).  
> 
> I also want to network this with my other systems which means UCX 
> I believe.  Does this mean that I want the "OpenVMS Alpha Software 
> Layered Products and Operating Systems Library"?  As I understand 
> it this includeds the media kits for VMS itself.
> 
> Is my assumption that the licenses are separate from the media kit?  
> If someone could give me some idea of the cost I would really 
> appreciate it.

        In the recent past, Alphastations with VMS have shipped the Base
    Alpha-VMS  license,  a  1-User  Alpha-VMS  license,  and  a  NAS-150
    license.   The last one gets you networking (UCX and DECnet) as well
    as DECwindows/Motif, which you need.

        While the media kit  probably  will be reasonable (more-or-less)
    since  it's  just a CDROM, I'd expect the Base VMS, 1-User  VMS  and
    NAS-150 to set you back a pretty penny, _far_ in excess of what  you
    paid  for the system.  You'll have to decide for yourself if you can
    afford or whether to just run Linux on the thing...  :-(

        FWIW, I'll never buy a  system  without the VMS and NAS licenses
    included.   In  the  refurbished market, you can  usually  get  both
    hardware and the required licenses at a reasonable price because the
    Base VMS, NAS-150, and I think,  1-User  VMS  licenses  can  all  be
    transferred  (by  the  seller)  for a single $300 fee so long as the
    original system  was  purchased  with  those  licenses.   A la carte
    license  prices  are crazy, like from another planet  (maybe  that's
    where the marketing folks are from too...)!  Without  the  licenses,
    the system is next to worthless (except for Linux aficionados).

        -Ken
